! BOSS App — v0.8.1 OSPF sidecar config, NODE role (3 WireGuard VPN nodes
! only: wireguard/wireguard-node2/wireguard-node3).
!
! This node redistributes STATIC routes (never `connected`) into OSPF —
! see docker/frr/handshake-watcher.sh for what actually creates/removes
! those static routes (conditional on a genuinely live WireGuard tunnel,
! covering every prefix in a peer's own AllowedIPs — the /30 router
! address AND any TR-069/OLT management subnets behind it, not just the
! router address alone). A `redistribute connected` variant was tried and
! abandoned for the subnet-prefix case — see handshake-watcher.sh's own
! header comment for the full "kernel route vs connected route vs static
! route, and why only removing the old bash-level `ip route replace` in
! docker/wireguard/entrypoint.sh actually fixed it" investigation.
!
! __HELLO__ / __DEAD__ / __AUTH_KEY__ / __ROUTER_ID__ are substituted by
! entrypoint.sh at container start (per-deployment values, not baked into
! the image).
hostname frr-node
log stdout
!
router ospf
 ospf router-id __ROUTER_ID__
 redistribute static route-map OSPF-WG-ONLY
!
! Tag-based (not prefix-list) — a NAS's TR-069/OLT management subnet can
! be any private range an ISP happens to use (10.1.0.0/20,
! 10.168.100.0/24 for test-x86-bajastu specifically, but not a fixed
! boundary for every future NAS), so filtering by fixed numeric range
! would silently exclude a future NAS's differently-ranged subnet.
! Tagging ties the permission to "was this route actually created by
! handshake-watcher.sh" (which tags every route it installs with
! `tag 100`), staying correct regardless of what ranges any given NAS's
! AllowedIPs happens to contain.
route-map OSPF-WG-ONLY permit 10
 match tag 100
!
interface eth0
 ip ospf hello-interval __HELLO__
 ip ospf dead-interval __DEAD__
 ip ospf authentication message-digest
 ip ospf message-digest-key 1 md5 __AUTH_KEY__
 ip ospf network broadcast
!
router ospf
 network 172.28.0.0/24 area 0.0.0.0
!
line vty
!
